  <h1 class="hidetitle">Titan Transients and LLM Scalability</h1>
  <h2>The ghost in the machine</h2>
  <h3>Neil J. Gunther</h3> 
  <p>While training LLMs, OpenAI observed what it termed the compute-efficient frontier (CEF), which characterizes a lower bound on token-prediction error or test loss. This limit has raised concerns that it might constitute a universal constraint on the scalability of GenAI systems. This article presents a model of LLM computational dynamics, based on the universal scalability law (USL), that provides a framework for understanding the CEF. The USL defines bistable minima in the tokenized neural net landscape. The deeper minimum determines the lowest achievable loss of an LLM instance. Successively larger LLMs possess deeper global minima that align with the power-law slope of the CEF. This suggests the CEF is a consequence of larger LLMs detecting inter-corpora correlations on all size scales.</p> 
  <p>In the course of training LLMs, an apparent limit on the achievable reduction in text prediction error has been observed by OpenAI (OAI) researchers.<sup>9</sup> This unexpected constraint—referred to as the <i>compute-efficient frontier</i> (CEF), a term possibly borrowed from the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efficient_frontier">fintech lexicon</a>—has raised concerns that it might constitute a fundamental limit on the scalability of GenAI systems. This article presents a model of LLM computational dynamics that provides a framework for understanding this newly observed constraint.</p> 
  <p>The CEF refers to the diagonal red line in figure 1. OAI authors have stated: “At present we do not have a solid theoretical understanding for any of our proposed scaling laws.”<sup>9</sup> Indeed, the OAI benchmarks have caused others in the GenAI community to remark: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5eqRuVp65eY">“AI models can’t cross this boundary and we don’t know why,”</a> and “Have we discovered <a href="https://x.com/hamptonism/status/1827087356999975270">a fundamental law of nature</a> for building intelligent systems?” Let’s address these concerns in two stages:</p> 
  <p>1. Each simulated loss curve in figure 1 is determined by a theoretical LLM cost function, labeled <i>K</i> in table 1 to avoid confusion with OAI’s use of <i>C</i> to denote compute.</p> 
  <p>2. The CEF arises from the increasing depth of the global minimum (<i>K<sub>b</sub></i> in table 1) belonging to each cost function as the size, <i>N</i>, of successive LLMs is increased.</p> 
