  </div> 
  <p>More formally, the CEF corresponds to power-law behavior.<sup>6</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>11</sup> That terminology stems from a curve such as <i>y = x<sup>−</sup></i><sup>1</sup> <i>= 1/x</i> appearing as a straight line when plotted on double logarithmic axes. Transforming to log axes is equivalent to taking the logarithm of both sides: <i>log y = − log x</i>. A general power law, <i>y = r x<sup>−s</sup></i>, includes constants <i>r</i> and <i>s</i>, where <i>s</i> is the exponent or power of <i>x</i>. Hence, the name. The magnitude of <i>s</i> determines the slope of the line while its sign determines the direction. In figure 1, <i>−s</i> means <i>Log-y</i> decreases as <i>Log-x</i> increases, and <i>r</i> is the <i>Log-y</i> intercept.</p> 
  <p>Empirical power laws often signal that a complex system undergoes some kind of macroscopic state transition caused by the onset of strong correlations.<sup>4</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>6</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>10</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>11</sup> Based on the benchmark measurements reported for OpenAI’s ChatGPT, a queue-theoretic model of LLM dynamics is constructed that exhibits power-law behavior corresponding to the CEF constraint. In particular, the prefactor <i>r</i> represents the scale of the LLM neural network, while the magnitude of <i>s</i> indicates the strength of scale-free text correlations.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>GPT Dynamics</h3> 
  <p>Before delving into the details of this analysis, let’s briefly review those aspects of GPT models that will be pertinent to what follows. The abbreviation GPT has swiftly become such common currency that many readers might not recall what the letters represent. Simply put:</p> 
  <p>– <i><b>G</b>enerative</i>&nbsp;(G) refers to the LLM being capable of responding to an input textual prompt with new output text that is considered coherent and credible by a typical human reader.</p> 
  <p>– <i><b>P</b>retrained</i>&nbsp;(P) refers to using a large curated corpus of encoded (English) text tokens to iteratively improve the LLM responses by comparing its predicted output against a known input.</p> 
  <p>– <i><b>T</b>ransformer</i>&nbsp;(T) refers to a particular kind of massively parallel, multilayer (feed-forward) neural network (NN) with trillions of neural connections (LLM parameters). NN nodes compute and update encoded token probabilities during a training run.</p> 
  <p>The transformer is the pattern-recognition subsystem that lies at the heart of all LLMs. Not only can it generate human-acceptable text responses, but it can also be applied to nontextual objects such as binary-format audio and video inputs.</p> 
  <p>The capabilities of GPT models really burst onto the scene when version 4 of ChatGPT was released in 2024. GPT-4 comprises approximately 2 trillion NN connections, or LLM parameters, whereas GPT-3 had just a measly 200 billion. As a consequence, any GPT-3 prompt produced only a single readable sentence. GPT-4o, however, with its gargantuan NN model, together with a massive amount of curated Internet data for LLM training, is capable of producing multiple paragraphs of text assembled into what is generally perceived (by human readers) to constitute coherent information. The empirical analysis by OAI considered here is based on version 3 of ChatGPT.<sup>9</sup></p> 
  <p>Because the total computational work needed to make accurate textual predictions is significant, LLMs are usually executed on specialized hardware, e.g., GPUs, rather than general-purpose multicore processors alone. While processing encoded input tokens during each training run, the weights of the NN connections are iteratively updated and a corresponding loss function is reevaluated. The loss is defined as the difference between the predicted value and the actual value—typically expressed as the <i>mean squared error</i>.</p> 
  <p>The upper part of figure 1 shows a set of simulated LLM training curves similar to those analyzed by OAI. Each curve corresponds to the discrete history of loss updates across successively larger LLMs. The inset shows the full sigmoidal shape of the simulated loss curves. (More on that aspect later.) An impressive amount of empirical data was collected by OAI over very long test durations. The performance metric on the y-axis (Test Loss) is a function of the amount of computational work on the x-axis that is consumed during each test run (measured in PF-Days). The training goal is to reduce the loss and thereby improve the predicted text sequence of an LLM instance.</p> 
  <p>It is clear from figure 1, however, that after a certain amount of computational effort by each LLM instance, there is no improvement in the Test Loss metric. Consequently, larger LLM instances with more NN connections are applied in an attempt to reduce Test Loss further. This results in loss curves that look like hockey sticks since they develop short “feet” at the CEF line. Beyond that line, the loss remains constant until the test is terminated.</p> 
  <p>OAI’s empirical analysis is principally based on the 2D representation of figure 1. The theoretical approach presented here introduces a 3D representation. Table 1 provides a glossary to aid the reader when comparing OAI’s 2D notation with the 3D notation used here, which is based on the universal scalability law (USL).<sup>4</sup></p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Bistable Queues</h3> 
  <p>Every system administrator is familiar with monitoring computer system performance based on the operating system run-queue, because shorter response times are generally associated with shorter run-queues. Performance metrics such as load average<sup>5</sup> quantify load in terms of the average run-queue length <i>(N)</i>, which can assist in developing intuition about what constitutes an optimal queue size or system load. It will therefore come as a surprise that certain queues can have more than one stable size: a bistable queue.<sup>1</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>3</sup></p> 
  <p>Figure 2 shows how a bistable queue can form. Processes arrive from the right-hand side and wait in the queue (depicted as red blocks) until they can be serviced at a processor (depicted as a blue disk). Serviced processes depart to the left. The two curves represent the average arrival rate (red line) and the average departure rate (blue curve).</p> 
  <img src="https://spawn-queue.acm.org/cms/10.1145/3819082/asset/6c613cc8-ada3-4312-8508-e8d5f01e7dc4/assets/graphic/gunther_fig2.svg" alt="Figure 2. A bistable queue with associated arrival and departure curves" />
  <br /><small>Figure 2. A bistable queue with associated arrival and departure curves</small> 
  <p>The arrival rate decreases linearly with <i>N</i> because there are only a finite number of requests that circulate between the queue and the load generators in the system. The queue is closed with respect to any external requests. If all <i>N</i> possible processes are enqueued, the arrival rate effectively falls to zero. The departure rate varies nonlinearly with <i>N</i> and is known as a load-dependent throughput,<sup>5</sup> which is denoted here by <i>X(N)</i>. The chief characteristic of <i>X(N)</i> is that it can become retrograde beyond a certain load (e.g., <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> in figure 2). This kind of queueing behavior is familiar to performance engineers running benchmarks or load tests, where diminishing throughput at high loads is not uncommon.</p> 
  <p>What do the curves in figure 2 indicate? First, they intersect each other at the dashed vertical lines. The leftmost vertical line at load <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> corresponds to a relatively short average queue length. If a queue fluctuation makes it shorter than <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> , arrivals exceed departures, whereby the queue will tend to grow back toward <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> rather than shrink further. Conversely, if arrivals cause the queue to grow, departures exceed arrivals, and the queue will again tend to shrink back toward <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> . Over a sufficiently long measurement period, the queue would be seen to fluctuate about the stable point <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> —the optimal queue length.</p> 
  <p>Second, the vertical line on the far right of figure 2, located at <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> , corresponds to a relatively long average queue length. Comparing the curves in the same way, you find that the queue exhibits the same behavior as for <i>N<sub>o</sub></i>, but for a longer average queue size. That means there is a long queue fluctuating around <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> that is also stable. In other words, this queue has two stable queue lengths.</p> 
  <p>Finally, what about the middle vertical line at <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> ? If the fluctuations are shorter than <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> , the queue will tend to shrink back toward <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> . If, however, any fluctuation is slightly larger than <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> , the queue will tend to grow toward <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> , which is also stable. <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> corresponds to a point of instability in the system.<sup>1</sup></p> 
  <p>The whole system, however, is said to be metastable, because, while it might spend an arbitrary amount of time fluctuating around <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> , it will eventually find its way to the other stable point <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> —the long, congested queue length—and possibly remain there for an intolerably long time. Therefore, <i>“b”</i> is for bad. Anyone who has experienced virtual memory thrashing knows what this condition feels like. Virtual memory is bistable.</p> 
  <p>The curves in figure 2 are continuous, whereas the enqueued requests are discrete. In queueing-model simulations, time is also discrete. Even if time is taken to be continuous in this discussion, how can continuous queue lengths be realistic? As long as the queue size is large and the times are long, the continuous approximation is valid. As already noted for ChatGPT, training data typically involves billions of encoded tokens, and training times are measured in days.</p> 
  <p>Indeed, just looking at the loss curves in figure 1, although they are constructed from discrete measurements, they essentially look continuous.</p> 
  <p>As intriguing as this bistability phenomenon is, mentally juggling the proximity of the rates is irksome. Luckily, there is a way to visualize this bistable queue more easily than figure 2:</p> 
  <p>– Starting with the original rate curves in figure 3(a), you take their difference to produce the single curve shown in figure 3(b).</p> 
  <p>– This curve is known as the <i>drift</i>.<sup>3</sup> The original points of intersection in figure 3(a) are now represented by the three points of intersection of the continuous drift function with the N-axis. Those points align with the vertical lines between figures 3(a) and 3(b).</p> 
  <p>– Taking the negative integral of the drift produces the cost function in figure 3(c), denoted <i>K(N)</i> in table 1. The stability points are now identified with a local minimum at <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> , the central maximum at <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> , and the global minimum <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> located at <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> .<sup>3</sup></p> 
  <img src="https://spawn-queue.acm.org/cms/10.1145/3819082/asset/69d5b8d3-caea-4c34-894d-37b077e1ec11/assets/graphic/gunther_fig3.svg" alt="Figure 3. Relationship between bistable Rates (a), Drift (b), and Cost (c)" /> 
  <br /> <small>Figure 3. Relationship between bistable Rates (a), Drift (b), and Cost (c)</small> 
  <p>It is now easy to visualize the growth of the queue tail as being like an imaginary ball bearing rolling from the upper valley at <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> in figure 3(c), over the hill at <i>N<sub>c</sub>,</i> and down into the lower valley at <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> .</p> 
  <p>Although the nonlinear form of the throughput in figures 2 and 3(a) is unusual, it is, in fact, a manifestation of the USL throughput that previously appeared in <a href="https://queue.acm.org/detail.cfm?id=2789974"><i>acmqueue</i></a> to analyze superlinear scaling.<sup>7</sup> There, <i>N</i> represented the number of Hadoop cluster nodes (hardware) running a fixed number of software processes per node. In the case of virtual memory thrashing,<sup>1</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>3</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>4</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>5</sup> <i>N</i> represents the number of (software) processes executing on a fixed hardware configuration. Equation 1 can be applied in either case because all the details of the actual computing platform are encoded in the three coefficients: α, β, and γ, which respectively represent the magnitudes of queueing contention, data coherence, and the level of concurrency in the system.<sup>4</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>8</sup></p> 
