  <p>The USL formula (equation 1) can be derived from an underlying single-queue model that exhibits precisely the same form of nonlinear throughput seen in figures 2 and 3(a). Moreover, event-based simulations have demonstrated (theorem 2)<sup>8</sup> that the nonlinear throughput can occur when the waiting line is first sorted before requests are actually serviced.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Titan Transients</h3> 
  <p>With the phenomenon of bistable queueing as background, let’s return to LLMs and the CEF. For a given LLM instance, the USL cost function <i>K(N)</i> in figure 3(c) now represents the amount of processing performed by the NN as it adjusts its connection weights against the input tokens. By analogy with the previous section, you can still think of LLM processing as being represented by a queue serviced by GPUs rather than CPUs. Only the nature of the computational work is different. Analogous&nbsp;to load dependence being caused by sorting the waiting line prior to service in equation 1, the NN graph can be transformed into a topologically sorted linear list.</p> 
  <p>Something that is not anticipated, however, is the following role reversal. <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> no longer corresponds to an optimal LLM state (as it did in the previous section), because relatively few tokens have been processed in the early phase of LLM training. Rather, <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> represents an origin state where tokens are mostly uncorrelated. Even if <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> is temporarily stable, nothing is achieved by remaining in a state where the loss function is still very high. Similarly, <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> is no longer the “bad” state. It is the new optimum, where the loss function is expected to be lowest at the end of the training run. <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> is now the boundary state of the CEF. This reversal in labeling becomes clearer in the next section.</p> 
  <p>To make contact with figure 1, the time evolution of any LLM loss curve, from <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> to <i>N<sub>b,</sub></i> needs to be determined. That requires introducing an explicit time coordinate. This is done as follows. There are only three kinds of performance metrics, defined in table 2. Any canonical or custom performance metric must belong to one of those types. Consider the OAI Compute metric, measured in units of <i>PF-Days</i> in figure 1. The <i>P</i> is an abbreviation for <i>peta</i>—the SI prefix for 10<sup>15</sup>—and the <i>F</i> is an abbreviation for floating-point operations per second, or FLOPS. Thus, <i>PF</i> stands for 10<sup>15</sup> FLOPS. Row 3 in table 2 shows that the per-second (inverse time) in FLOPS means that <i>PF</i> must signify a rate metric.</p> 
