  <p><i>PF-Days</i> is shorthand for PFLOPS multiplied by time—in units of Days. The product of a rate metric (inverse time) and a time metric is a pure number metric (row 2), because the time metrics cancel. <i>PF-Days</i> is the total number of PFLOPs (plural) operations expended to reduce each Test-Loss curve in figure 1 to the point where it becomes a plateau at the CEF line. For reference, a single <i>PF-Day</i> is equivalent to <i>10<sup>15</sup> FLOPS × 24 hours × 3, 600 seconds = 8.64 × 10<sup>19</sup></i> floating-point operations, or 86.4 exaFLOPs; not exaFLOPS.</p> 
  <p>For this analysis, the time evolution of the loss transition is more important than the total number of operations expended (<i>n</i>). Ignoring the types of operations (floating point), you can write <i>1 k.day = 86.4 exa-ops</i>, where <i>k</i> is a constant. Therefore, <i>1 day = 86.4/k</i>, and the <i>n</i>-axis in figure 1 can be replaced by a time-axis (<i>T</i>), where <i>T = n/k</i>. This remapping is equivalent to Little’s law,<sup>5</sup> which is applicable, even during the large loss transition. Each test measurement, taken during the transition, has the same sample period <i>(∆T)</i> such that <i>∆n = k∆T</i> is valid. The procedure is no different from the way the linear-rising portion of throughput is measured, at low loads, in a conventional load test system.<sup>4</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>5</sup></p> 
  <p>The simple 2D curve in figure 3(c) makes it easy to visualize the drift of the queue size from <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> to <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> as an imaginary ball bearing rolling between the two valleys. But queues are stochastic, not deterministic, so it cannot really behave as a ball bearing. Or, can it? It is more accurate to think of the queue fluctuations in the upper valley as being like those of a tiny dust particle jiggling around the stable point <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> . Think of the imaginary dust particle as being stuck on the tail of the queue. Similarly, the long stable queue would correspond to the dust particle jiggling around <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> . But how can a jiggling dust particle get over the hill at <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> ?</p> 
  <p>Depending on the exact hill profile, successive tiny jiggles are unlikely to get the dust particle up and over that hill. Here comes the next surprise. The dust particle suddenly appears to morph into a ball bearing, rolls up and over the hill and down into the lower valley (as before), where it reverts to being a jiggling dust particle again. In other words, it is not a series of tiny fluctuations that causes the queue to grow beyond <i>N<sub>c</sub></i> , but a sudden, giant fluctuation: a <i>titan transient</i>.</p> 
  <p>The 3D time-evolution of the LLM processing queue is shown in figure 4. The titan transient follows a sigmoidal path that is determined by the shape of the cost surface, <i>K(N)</i>. The morphing dust particle analogy captures the notion that the titan transient becomes manifest because of the sudden onset of strong correlations during the loss computations. Incidentally, the titan transient in figure 4 is plotted on linear axes, whereas the loss curves in figure 1 are plotted on log axes. Changing to log axes has the effect of stretching the upper part of figure 4b and squashing the bottom part, making the transition appear somewhat steeper in figure 1.</p> 
