  <p>The empirical loss curve is the result of the <i>NN</i> “sniffing out” the unknown cost landscape defined by the training data (e.g., via gradient descent). The USL model represents a simple, a priori theoretical cost landscape. The resulting transients, however, have a similar sigmoidal shape to those observed by OAI. That suggests the USL cost landscape is not too different from the OAI empirical landscape. Furthermore, the USL model accounts for the longer time spent by larger LLMs in the <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> origin state. Successive loss curves are shifted to the right in figure 1 because it takes longer for significant correlations to begin emerging in larger LLMs.</p> 
  <p>The gradient descent algorithm can also be understood from figure 3. It searches for the minimum mean-square error. A minimum occurs at the bottom of a valley in figure 3(c), where the gradient is zero. The gradient is the derivative of <i>K</i>, which is equivalent to the drift in figure 3(b). Thus, the global minimum at <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> corresponds to the rightmost N-intersection point of the drift function. Of course, the LLM computational algorithm is the discrete version of this procedure.</p> 
  <p>In the applied statistics and queueing literature, such titan transients are known as <i>large deviations</i>.<sup>2</sup> Because of the mathematical subtleties associated with the cost function, <i>K</i>, the time evolution of the titan transient in figure 4 is calculated numerically.<sup>6</sup> As a general matter, titan transients have a relatively low probability of occurring immediately in conventional computer systems, because the enqueued processes are only weakly correlated. When a large transient suddenly does emerge, it appears like an unexpected ghost out of nowhere. Similarly for LLMs, the loss is initially high because the tokens are weakly correlated at the beginning of a training run. Eventually, as a result of the LLM computations, the sudden onset of stronger correlations drives the loss directly toward the boundary state at <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> .</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>CEF Bound</h3> 
  <p>So far, this article has established that each empirical loss curve in figure 1 can be understood as arising from the sudden onset of a large transient that is determined by an associated cost function shown in figure 4. That completes stage 1 of this analysis. To see how titan transients are related to the CEF, let’s now consider successively larger LLM instances.</p> 
  <p>Attempting to include many LLM instances would obscure the 3D visualization with too much clutter. To avoid that, consider the global minima of just three exemplar LLMs that are each separated by a decade in size on the <i>Log-N</i> axis (i.e., <i>N<sub>b</sub> = 10<sup>7</sup>, 10<sup>8</sup>, 10<sup>9</sup></i> in figure 5). Each global minimum, <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> , of the USL cost function is depicted as a truncated valley that is now extended into the third dimension of increasing time, i.e., the Log-T axis.</p> 
  <img src="https://spawn-queue.acm.org/cms/10.1145/3819082/asset/28594dd4-ffbb-42e2-b258-7c826fc83a21/assets/graphic/gunther_fig5.svg" alt="Figure 5. Global minima belonging to three exemplar LLM models" /> 
  <br /> <small>Figure 5. Global minima belonging to three exemplar LLM models</small> 
  <p>Each <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> in figure 5 is a 3D version of the 2D lower valley at <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> in figure 3(c). To keep track of the orientation of these three minima, a red line is placed along the bottom of each valley. Each red line sits on the inclined plane in figure 5. When viewed directly through the <i>Log-KN</i> axes, it corresponds to the diagonal CEF line in figure 1.</p> 
  <p>The meaning of the CEF power-law bound can now be understood as follows. By rotating figure 5 about the vertical <i>Log-K</i> axis, through 90 degrees to the left, the <i>Log-T</i> axis swings around to replace the <i>Log-N</i> axis. The resulting perspective is shown in figure 6, where the three red lines in figure 5 now appear as three parallel lines that are constant in time.</p> 
  <img src="https://spawn-queue.acm.org/cms/10.1145/3819082/asset/9893ef06-1790-40f1-9d9b-e621e4917ab0/assets/graphic/gunther_fig6.svg" alt="Figure 6. View through the Log-KT axes of figure 5" /> 
  <br /> <small>Figure 6. View through the Log-KT axes of figure 5</small> 
  <p>Figure 6 depicts three exemplar titan transients that have reached their respective red lines as blue “hockey-stick” curves with arrows pointing in the direction of increasing time. Titan transients, belonging to LLMs that are intermediate in size to the three exemplar transients, are depicted as thin blue lines. Although no red lines are shown for those intermediate transients, they have the same hockey-stick shape as they reach the dashed CEF line in figure 6. Collectively, these blue hockey sticks correspond to the simulated loss curves in figure 1.</p> 
  <p>The reason why the CEF has remained so puzzling now becomes clear. Figure 1 is the “flattened” version of figure 6, with the horizontal red lines missing. The 2D view is ambiguous when trying to interpret figure 1. That situation is completely understandable. The red lines require locating the global minima, <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> , of each LLM instance, and each empirical cost function was not explicitly computed for the OAI training runs. The theoretical 3D visualization disambiguates the original 2D representation. Moreover, the plateaus in the loss curves at the CEF bound are also accounted for. This completes stage 2 of the analysis in this article.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Conclusion</h3> 
  <p>The foregoing analysis has provided a different perspective on the original question about the significance of the CEF. The evolution of each LLM loss-curve in figure 1 can be interpreted as corresponding to a monotonically decreasing transient that takes the LLM training system from an uncorrelated, high-loss state to a highly correlated, low-loss state. The time development of this large transient is controlled by a bistable queue, where the characteristics are determined by the coefficients of the USL cost surface. The phenomenon of bistable queueing has been studied in a variety of conventional computer systems<sup>1</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>3</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>4</sup> and is therefore not unique to LLMs.</p> 
  <p>Similarly, power-law behavior is also known in conventional computer systems and networks,<sup>4</sup><sup>,</sup><sup>10</sup> but the way it arises in LLMs is unusual. Each LLM has its own associated global minimum, <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> , corresponding to the deepest valley in figures 3(c) and 5. Each set of LLM parameters is rescaled by many orders of magnitude prior to the next training run. The degree of cumulative measurement needed to expose the CEF is exceptionally large compared with typical benchmarking systems, and that makes this aspect of LLM performance analysis uniquely difficult.</p> 
  <p>The CEF appears as an unexpected limit because recomputing already strongly correlated tokens does not lower the loss once it reaches the globally stable valley at <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> . In conventional computer systems, queueing is either random or weakly correlated in time, whereas the long queues are a result of the LLM detecting strong correlations. The LLM power-law phenomenon described here is reminiscent of the highly correlated packet trains observed in certain types of network traffic.<sup>10</sup> The striking difference is that, unlike conventional computer systems, strongly correlated “congestion” is the desired goal for LLMs. <i>N<sub>b</sub></i> , rather than <i>N<sub>o</sub></i> , is the performance optimum.</p> 
  <p>Further insight into the nature of the CEF comes from comparing it with Zipf’s law.<sup>11</sup> The canonical Zipf exponent is <i>s = 1.0</i>. The empirically determined LLM loss-exponent is <i>s = 0.05</i>.<sup>9</sup> The Zipf exponent derives from ranking (English) word frequencies sampled from a relatively small corpus compared with LLM training data. It characterizes correlations that are stronger than the exponential tails of standard statistical distributions. A fractional exponent, on the other hand, implies correlations that are much stronger than Zipf’s law. From this perspective, the much smaller CEF loss exponent suggests that LLMs detect <i>inter-corpora</i> correlations on all size-<i>N</i> scales: plausibly a new phenomenon in computer science.</p> 
  <p>Finally, the reader might be wondering why no effort has been made to incorporate a lot more detail regarding the GPT architecture beyond that briefly outlined in the introductory sections. The remarkable answer is that near a critical point (e.g., <i>N<sub>c</sub></i>) the system forgets what it is. Once the queue fluctuations suddenly become correlated enough to induce a titan transient, that timescale completely dominates the hierarchy of shorter timescales in the LLM computing platform.</p> 
